There's no existing project in this directory. I'll demonstrate the full implementation pattern. Here's what you need:

### 1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourApp.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException   => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // Let the default handler produce a generic 500

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title,  // Don't use exception.Message — may leak internals
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

### 2. Wire it up in `Program.cs`

```csharp
// --- Service registration ---
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();  // Formats unhandled (500) as Problem Details too

// --- Middleware pipeline (order matters — place early) ---
app.UseExceptionHandler();   // Catches exceptions, dispatches to IExceptionHandler
app.UseStatusCodePages();    // Converts bare status codes (e.g. 404) to Problem Details
```

### How it works

| Layer | Responsibility |
|-------|---------------|
| `ApiExceptionHandler` | Maps known exceptions → 400/404/409 with Problem Details |
| Default `UseExceptionHandler` | Catches anything `ApiExceptionHandler` returns `false` for → generic 500 Problem Details (no sensitive details) |
| `UseStatusCodePages` | Wraps non-exception error status codes (e.g. routing 404) in Problem Details |

No `try-catch` needed in endpoints — throw exceptions from your service layer and they're automatically mapped:

```csharp
app.MapGet("/api/products/{id}", async (int id, IProductService svc, CancellationToken ct) =>
{
    // If GetByIdAsync throws KeyNotFoundException → automatic 404 Problem Details
    var product = await svc.GetByIdAsync(id, ct);
    return TypedResults.Ok(product);
});
```

**Key points:**
- `Detail` uses a safe string, never `exception.Message`
- Returning `false` for unknown exceptions lets the built-in handler produce a clean 500
- `AddProblemDetails()` ensures the 500 response body is also RFC 7807 compliant
